<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Created: 27/09/2026 -->
<!--
  Interaction pole: a rigid mast rising from the floor with a flat vertical
  panel that a vehicle can push against.

  Frame convention. The model origin sits on the floor at the base of the
  mast, and the panel's contact face lies exactly in the local x = 0 plane
  facing -X. Placing the model at world x = 2.0 therefore puts the surface
  the vehicle touches at x = 2.0, with no need to account for panel or mast
  thickness. Rotate the model about Z to face the surface elsewhere.

  Geometry is ESTIMATED; nothing here is measured from laboratory hardware:
    base plate   0.35 m square, 0.02 m thick;
    mast         0.07 m diameter, 2.03 m tall;
    push panel   0.60 m wide, 1.20 m tall, 0.025 m thick, spanning
                 z = 0.80 m to z = 2.00 m, centred at z = 1.40 m.
  The panel height band brackets the usual 1.0-1.5 m hover height so the
  vehicle meets the surface, not an edge.

  The pole is an effectively immovable rigid obstacle, the usual reference
  condition for interaction-force control. Getting a force reading out of it
  dictates the link and joint structure, which is otherwise more elaborate than
  the shape needs. Two gz-sim constraints drive it, both established by
  experiment rather than from the documentation:

  1. A <static> model has no joints, and a force_torque sensor must live on a
     joint. A joint parented directly to `world` is also refused by the
     ForceTorque system ("Parent link with name [world] ... not found"). So the
     sensor needs a real link on each side: hence the otherwise pointless
     anchor link, welded to the world, with the pole hanging off it.

  2. A force_torque sensor on a joint does NOT report external contact loads
     when its chain is rigidly welded to the world. Welded bodies are merged
     into the world's infinite-mass body, which swallows the contact; the
     sensor then reports only the child's own weight and inertia. Measured: a
     10 kg box resting on a welded plate moved the reading not at all, staying
     at the plate's own -19.6 N instead of -117.6 N. Making one joint in the
     chain a stiff prismatic instead of a weld fixes it completely, and it then
     reports loads along every axis, not just the joint axis: the same test
     read -117.6 N exactly, and -117.6 N again with the joint axis turned
     perpendicular to the load.

  So pole_base is a prismatic joint along X, the panel normal, with a stiffness
  of 1e6 N/m. A 50 N push deflects it 50 um, which is rigid for any practical
  purpose and invisible on screen. To make the surface genuinely yield, drop
  the stiffness or change pole_base to revolute.

  None of this applies to the vehicle-side sensor in planar_octo_rod, whose rod
  joint is a plain weld: the vehicle is a free body, not welded to the world,
  and there the weld reports external contact correctly (measured -117.57 N in
  the same test with a free base).

  Two sensors, answering two different questions:

    .../link/pole/sensor/push_surface_contact/contact   WHERE contact happened
    .../joint/pole_base/sensor/reaction_force/forcetorque  HOW HARD

  The contact sensor reports point positions, normals and penetration depth.
  It does NOT report force: gz-sim leaves that message's wrench field at zero
  (checked against a 10 kg load that must carry 98 N; every component came
  back zero). Force comes only from the force_torque sensor, which reads a
  10 kg reference load as exactly 98.0 N.

  Both sensor systems must be loaded from PX4's server.config, and must NOT be
  added as a <plugin> in a world SDF: any world-level plugin in an SDF replaces
  the entire server.config plugin set, leaving a world with no physics, no
  sensors and no spawn service.

  PX4 never sees this model, so its force is logged host-side instead of into
  the ulog: Tools/dtrg/log_interaction_force.py writes it to CSV.

  Mass is ESTIMATED at 25 kg (steel base plate, steel mast, panel) with an
  estimated diagonal inertia. Because the link is welded to the world the
  inertia has almost no dynamic effect, but the weight does appear in the
  sensor reading as a constant offset; the CSV logger's --tare option removes
  it.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="interaction_pole">
    <static>false</static>

    <!-- Weld point. No geometry; it exists only to give the force sensor a
         parent link, since a joint parented to `world` cannot carry one. -->
    <link name="anchor">
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.001</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>1e-6</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyy>1e-6</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>1e-6</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="anchor_to_world" type="fixed">
      <parent>world</parent>
      <child>anchor</child>
    </joint>

    <link name="pole">
      <inertial>
        <pose>0.045 0 1.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>25.0</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>8.4</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyy>8.4</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.9</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>

      <!-- Floor plate. -->
      <collision name="base_plate_collision">
        <pose>0.060 0 0.010 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.35 0.35 0.02</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="base_plate_visual">
        <pose>0.060 0 0.010 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.35 0.35 0.02</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.16 0.17 0.18 1</ambient><diffuse>0.26 0.27 0.28 1</diffuse><specular>0.30 0.30 0.30 1</specular></material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Mast, behind the panel so the panel face stays at local x = 0. -->
      <collision name="mast_collision">
        <pose>0.060 0 1.025 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.035</radius><length>2.01</length></cylinder></geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="mast_visual">
        <pose>0.060 0 1.025 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.035</radius><length>2.01</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.22 1</ambient><diffuse>0.32 0.33 0.34 1</diffuse><specular>0.28 0.28 0.28 1</specular></material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Push panel. Contact face at local x = 0, normal pointing -X. -->
      <collision name="push_surface_collision">
        <pose>0.0125 0 1.400 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.60 1.20</size></box></geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction><ode><mu>0.7</mu><mu2>0.7</mu2></ode></friction>
          <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>60</kd></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="push_surface_visual">
        <pose>0.0125 0 1.400 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.60 1.20</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.58 0.60 0.63 1</ambient><diffuse>0.76 0.78 0.81 1</diffuse><specular>0.20 0.20 0.20 1</specular></material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Aiming target on the panel face, at the panel centre (z = 1.4 m). -->
      <visual name="target_ring">
        <pose>-0.0005 0 1.400 0 1.57079632679 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.13</radius><length>0.001</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.55 0.18 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.34 0.08 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="target_centre">
        <pose>-0.0011 0 1.400 0 1.57079632679 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.06</radius><length>0.001</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.23 1</ambient><diffuse>0.30 0.31 0.34 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Panel edge banding, so the usable surface is visible from a distance. -->
      <visual name="panel_edge_positive_y"><pose>0.0005 0.285 1.400 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.026 0.03 1.20</size></box></geometry><material><ambient>0.55 0.18 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.34 0.08 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <visual name="panel_edge_negative_y"><pose>0.0005 -0.285 1.400 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.026 0.03 1.20</size></box></geometry><material><ambient>0.55 0.18 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.34 0.08 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <visual name="panel_edge_top"><pose>0.0005 0 1.985 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.026 0.60 0.03</size></box></geometry><material><ambient>0.55 0.18 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.34 0.08 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <visual name="panel_edge_bottom"><pose>0.0005 0 0.815 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.026 0.60 0.03</size></box></geometry><material><ambient>0.55 0.18 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.34 0.08 1</diffuse></material></visual>

      <sensor name="push_surface_contact" type="contact">
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <contact><collision>push_surface_collision</collision></contact>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!--
      Instrumented mount. Stiff rather than welded, for the reason given in the
      header. The sensor measures the wrench the pole delivers into its anchor,
      and so into the floor, expressed in the pole frame, which is world
      aligned when the model is spawned at yaw 0.

      Sign, verified in simulation: the pole's own weight appears as a constant
      force z of -245.0 N, exactly 25 kg x 9.8, with a torque y of 11.025 N m
      from the 45 mm centre-of-mass offset. A vehicle pushing the panel in +X
      adds a POSITIVE force x. Tare the logger to drop the standing offset and
      read the push on its own.
    -->
    <joint name="pole_base" type="prismatic">
      <parent>anchor</parent>
      <child>pole</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>1 0 0</xyz>
        <limit><lower>-0.005</lower><upper>0.005</upper></limit>
        <dynamics>
          <spring_stiffness>1000000</spring_stiffness>
          <spring_reference>0</spring_reference>
          <damping>5000</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
      <sensor name="reaction_force" type="force_torque">
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>250</update_rate>
        <force_torque>
          <frame>child</frame>
          <measure_direction>child_to_parent</measure_direction>
        </force_torque>
      </sensor>
    </joint>
  </model>
</sdf>
